## Evidence / Ops

Task-to-tool routing only. The full contract — package shape, gate timeline
of who writes what when, manifest, `commands.log` telemetry, verdicts,
eligibility, and waivers — lives in `agents/docs/AGENT-OPS.md`.

Honor `{NEBULA_PRODUCT_ROOT}/.agentignore` before broad product discovery. Treat
`{NEBULA_PRODUCT_ROOT}/planning-mds/operations/**` as cold archive: start from
`operations/evidence/README.md`, feature `latest-run.json`, and the selected
run's `evidence-manifest.json`; read raw reports, logs, screenshots, and
`artifacts/**` only when the current audit, validation, or failure-triage task
requires those exact files. Full retrieval semantics live in
`agents/docs/AGENTIGNORE.md`.

| When | Command |
|------|---------|
| Validate evidence mid-run (G0–G5) | `python3 agents/product-manager/scripts/validate-feature-evidence.py --product-root {NEBULA_PRODUCT_ROOT} --feature F#### --run-id <run-id> --stage <Gn>` |
| Final closeout validation | `python3 agents/product-manager/scripts/validate-feature-evidence.py --product-root {NEBULA_PRODUCT_ROOT} --feature F#### --stage closeout` (see AGENT-OPS.md for the stage matrix) |
| Closeout supersession (before writing `latest-run.json`) | `python3 agents/product-manager/scripts/patch-prior-manifest.py --product-root {NEBULA_PRODUCT_ROOT} --feature F#### --new-run-id <run-id>` |
| Run lifecycle gates (writes `lifecycle-gates.log`) | `python3 agents/scripts/run-lifecycle-gates.py` |
| New run artifact / report | copy the matching skeleton from `agents/templates/` (see AGENT-OPS.md → Package Anatomy) |
     1	# SOLUTION-PATTERNS.md — Nebula Insurance Brain
     2	
     3	Project-level implementation conventions every role follows. Seeded by the Architect at F0001 Phase B from the master blueprint and the accepted ADRs; changes go through an ADR first.
     4	
     5	## Metadata
     6	
     7	- Project: Nebula Insurance Brain
     8	- Version: 0.1
     9	- Last Updated: 2026-09-28 (F0002: authorization enforcement point, ADR-0062)
    10	- Owners: Architect (patterns), Security (pattern 1 co-sign)
    11	- Scope: `engine/`, `neuron/`, `experience/` (from F0021), runtime assets, contracts under `planning-mds/`
    12	
    13	## Pattern Categories
    14	
    15	- `MUST` - mandatory convention for this solution
    16	- `SHOULD` - recommended convention unless a documented exception exists
    17	- `MAY` - optional convention based on feature needs
    18	
    19	## Pattern Scope Labels
    20	
    21	- `Universal` - technology-agnostic rule expected in any stack
    22	- `Stack-Specific` - tied to a concrete framework/tool choice
    23	- `Hybrid` - universal policy with stack-specific implementation details
    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	## 1. Authorization Pattern
    28	
    29	### Scope
    30	- Label: `Hybrid`
    31	- Stack context: authentik OIDC, native Casbin adapter in `brain_security`, PostgreSQL-backed principals and memberships
    32	
    33	### Decision
    34	- Policy model: verified `(issuer, subject)` → stable internal principal; structural tenancy (tenant, knowledge base) resolved from current grants; Casbin evaluates `(role, resource type, action, condition)` over server-hydrated typed attributes; authorization is the conjunction of membership, action, resource or parent scope, classification, and delegation limits.
    35	- Enforcement point: every API route authenticates through `brain_api.deps.current_principal` and runs its protected operation through `brain_security.execution.AuthorizationExecution` (`read` / `mutate`), which locks current authority, evaluates with the pure `brain_security.evaluation.evaluate`, and commits the decision audit before any payload or together with the mutation. Background workers use the same `execution.decide` orchestration over a sync store. No consumer assembles its own resource snapshot. Commit services run inside the facade's unit of work. Search, vector, and graph paths filter before rows, counts, facets, and snippets; MCP uses the same path.
    36	
    37	### Rationale
    38	- Master blueprint sections 66, 118 to 120; ADR-0030; proposed ADR-0049, ADR-0050, ADR-0053. The CRM gaps in section 119 are not copied.
    39	
    40	### Applied In
    41	- Backend: `brain_security` (verifier, identity profile, resolver, Casbin adapter, evaluator, execution facade, delegation rules, audit); trusted state in `brain_persistence` (`authx`, `tenancy`, `identity`, `grants`); routes in `brain_api.routes.*`
    42	- Frontend: renders what the API returns; never derives permissions client-side (F0021)
    43	- AI layer: `neuron/` calls the engine as the acting principal; the model server receives no identity data
    44	
    45	### Enforcement Level
    46	- `MUST`
    47	
    48	### Example
    49	
    50	```text
    51	p, TenantMember, content_artifact, read, r.obj.knowledge_base_id == r.sub.knowledge_base_id
    52	p, Reviewer, review_task, annotate, r.obj.knowledge_base_id == r.sub.knowledge_base_id
    53	p, ServicePrincipal, fact_slot, commit, r.obj.knowledge_base_id == r.sub.knowledge_base_id
    54	```
    55	
    56	Denials are reported as 404; reason codes live only in `audit_event`.
    57	
    58	---
    59	
    60	## 2. Audit and Timeline Pattern
    61	
    62	### Scope
    63	- Label: `Hybrid`
    64	- Stack context: PostgreSQL append-only tables written inside the same transaction as the change
    65	
    66	### Decision
    67	- What gets logged: every authorization decision (policy hash, grant revision, actor and delegate, resource, action, decision, reason code, trace id); every canonical commit (commit id, fact versions, change reason, evidence); every review decision; every interpretation run.
    68	- Event immutability policy: `audit_event`, `outbox_event`, `canonical_fact_change`, `review_event`, and `review_decision` are append-only; corrections create new rows linked to the originals (ADR-0037).
    69	
    70	### Rationale
    71	- Sections 17, 66, 109.3; ADR-0009, ADR-0010, ADR-0037; proposed ADR-0041.
    72	
    73	### Applied In
    74	- Data model: `audit_event`, `outbox_event`, `canonical_fact_change`, `review_event`
    75	- Workflow engine: none in v0.1; Temporal (F0050) proposes state changes only through commit services (ADR-0028)
    76	
    77	### Enforcement Level
    78	- `MUST`
    79	
    80	### Example
    81	
    82	```json
    83	{"actor_principal_id": "…", "resource_type": "fact_slot", "action": "commit", "decision": "allow", "policy_hash": "sha256:…", "grant_revision": 7, "trace_id": "…"}
    84	```
    85	
    86	---
    87	
    88	## 3. API Design Pattern
    89	
    90	### Scope
    91	- Label: `Hybrid`
    92	- Stack context: FastAPI, OpenAPI 3.1 contract in `planning-mds/api/brain-api.yaml`, JSON Schema 2020-12 in `planning-mds/schemas/`
    93	
    94	### Decision
    95	- API style: REST over root resource paths (`/content/{id}`, `/facts/{id}`, `/reviews/{id}`); no `/api` prefix; nouns only; versioning by OpenAPI `info.version` until a breaking change forces a path version.
    96	- Error format: RFC 9457 `ProblemDetails` with a stable `code` and a `traceId` extension member (the one camelCase field, required by the framework API contract validator), media type `application/problem+json`.
    97	- Pagination approach: cursor-based (`cursor`, `limit` up to 100) for list endpoints (none in F0001).
    98	- Time coordinates: `validAsOf` and `knownAsOf` query parameters on canonical reads, defaulting to now.
    99	
   100	### Rationale
   101	- Section 68; ADR-0013; the framework API validator rejects `/api` prefixes and requires ProblemDetails on error responses.
   102	
   103	### Applied In
   104	- API contracts: `planning-mds/api/brain-api.yaml`
   105	- Service endpoints: `brain_api.routes.*`
   106	
   107	### Enforcement Level
   108	- `MUST`
   109	
   110	### Example
   111	
   112	```http
   113	GET /facts/8b2c…?validAsOf=2026-07-01T00:00:00Z&knownAsOf=2026-06-05T00:00:00Z
   114	```
   115	
   116	---
